#4471 is marked known, with one fix queued: make the parser read the tenant's date order. That fix
closes only the first row below. The other two rows need their own fixes, and until now they had no
ticket.

<Callout kind="warn">
Do not close #4471 when the parser fix ships. Close each ticket below on its own fix.
</Callout>

## What #4471 holds

<ProblemTable ticket={4471} {issues} />

Each report is placed by its tenant, the booking form that tenant runs, and the channel the report
came in on (`causes.ts`, `problems.ts`). Intake tags and subject lines do not decide it.

## The three problems

### `parser`: dates read month first

The shared parser reads every date as MM/DD. Every tenant in #4471 writes the day first, so any date
with a day of 12 or less swaps: 7 April becomes 4 July. Reports come from every channel and every
tenant. This is the fix already queued under #4471.

### `hint`: booking-v3 tells day-first tenants to type MM/DD

The booking-v3 form prints `MM/DD/YYYY` under its date field. People type what the hint says. The form
then reads the date the tenant's way, day first, and the date swaps. The parser fix does not touch
this: the form needs its hint to follow the tenant's locale. Only form reports from booking-v3
tenants show it.

### `shift`: the date moves by one day

These reports are not a swap. Tuesday 12 May shows as Monday 11 May. A Lisbon client sees Tuesday and
a Toronto client sees Monday for the same meeting. One invite arrived before the booking was made.
That points at a time-zone conversion of the invite date, not at date order. The desk noted these
reports as a one-day move (`desk-notes.json`) and filed them under the parser by tenant. The table
counts them in both places, the way the desk counts, but only the `shift` ticket fixes them.

## Since April

<QueueTimeline ticket={4471} />

## Why one ticket hid three

- Decision [0001](/kb/decisions/0001-dedupe-by-subject) merges on the subject line. Reporters describe
  the symptom, "wrong day", and every problem here has the same symptom. The table above counts how many
  subject lines span more than one problem.
- Intake tags come from the reporter's browser, not from the tenant. They split the queue wrongly in
  both directions.
- A report that does not fit the headline (a one-day move is not a swap) was footnoted instead of
  getting a ticket.

## For the desk

- For date reports, place a report by tenant, form and channel before you merge it.
- A report whose symptom differs from the ticket's headline gets its own ticket, even if only a few
  reports share it.

